{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Calibration of a low-cost capacitive soil-moisture probe across three soil textures",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Priya Raman, Tomas Lindqvist, Amara Okafor",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Low-cost capacitive probes make dense soil-moisture networks affordable, but their output depends on soil texture and temperature. We calibrated 12 probes against oven-dry gravimetric measurements in a sand, a loam and a clay soil (144 samples in total). A quadratic relation between probe voltage and volumetric water content gave root-mean-square errors (RMSE) of 0.012, 0.015 and 0.021 m³ m⁻³ for the three soils, and 0.019 m³ m⁻³ for a single pooled model. Between 278 and 293 K the temperature drift stayed below 0.004 m³ m⁻³. Texture-specific calibration is therefore advisable when the error budget is below 0.02 m³ m⁻³.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Abstract"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "soil moisture · capacitive sensor · calibration · low-cost instrumentation · precision agriculture",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Keywords"
    },
    {
      "text": "\\linenumbers",
      "type": "RawLatex"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Irrigation scheduling needs soil-moisture data at the scale of a field, which means tens or hundreds of probes rather than one. Commercial time-domain reflectometry probes cost several hundred dollars each, while capacitive probes built around a 555 timer cost about 3 USD; their price has opened the door to dense networks ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "Nakamura2021",
            "Berg2019"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The cost is paid in accuracy: the factory calibration is a straight line between air and water, and the probes respond to soil bulk density, salinity and temperature as well as to water ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "Berg2019"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ".",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This paper answers a practical question: how much is gained by calibrating for each soil texture, compared with a single calibration for all soils? We describe the calibration procedure (Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "sec:methods",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": "), report the errors for three contrasting soils (Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "sec:results",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ") and discuss the temperature effect and the limits of the approach (Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "sec:discussion",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ").",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Materials and methods",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:methods",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Probe and soils",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Twelve identical probes were read with a 12-bit analogue-to-digital converter at 3.3 V. Three soils were sampled from the top 20 cm of experimental plots: a sand (92% sand), a loam (41% sand, 38% silt) and a clay (58% clay). Each soil was air-dried, sieved to 2 mm and packed to a bulk density of 1.35 g cm⁻³ in 1 L cylinders.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Calibration procedure",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Each cylinder was wetted to eight water contents between air-dry and saturation and equilibrated for 24 h. The probe voltage ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "V",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " was recorded for 5 min, after which a 20 mL core was weighed wet and oven-dried at 105 °C to obtain the volumetric water content ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\theta",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ". We fitted the quadratic model",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\theta = a_0 + a_1 V + a_2 V^{2}",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:calib",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "by least squares, either separately for each soil or for all 144 samples together. Goodness of fit is reported as the coefficient of determination ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "R^2",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " and the RMSE of ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:rmse",
          "kind": "eqref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\mathrm{RMSE} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\left(\\theta_i^{\\mathrm{model}} - \\theta_i^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right)^{2}}",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:rmse",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Temperature drift was measured by cycling the loam cylinders between 278 and 293 K in a climate chamber at constant water content.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:results",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:fit",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " lists the fit quality. All soils are described well by Eq. (",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:calib",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": "): ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "R^2",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " exceeds 0.95 everywhere and the bias is within ±0.003 m³ m⁻³. The error grows with clay content, from 0.012 m³ m⁻³ in the sand to 0.021 m³ m⁻³ in the clay. The pooled model has an RMSE of 0.019 m³ m⁻³.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
