          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "\\linenumbers",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "1 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Irrigation scheduling needs soil-moisture data at the scale of a field, which means tens or hundreds of probes rather than one. Commercial time-domain reflectometry probes cost several hundred dollars each, while capacitive probes built around a 555 timer cost about 3 USD; their price has opened the door to dense networks ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "[1, 2]",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The cost is paid in accuracy: the factory calibration is a straight line between air and water, and the probes respond to soil bulk density, salinity and temperature as well as to water ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "[2]",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ".",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This paper answers a practical question: how much is gained by calibrating for each soil texture, compared with a single calibration for all soils? We describe the calibration procedure (Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "), report the errors for three contrasting soils (Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "3",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ") and discuss the temperature effect and the limits of the approach (Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ").",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "2 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Materials and methods",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "2.1 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Probe and soils",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Twelve identical probes were read with a 12-bit analogue-to-digital converter at 3.3 V. Three soils were sampled from the top 20 cm of experimental plots: a sand (92% sand), a loam (41% sand, 38% silt) and a clay (58% clay). Each soil was air-dried, sieved to 2 mm and packed to a bulk density of 1.35 g cm⁻³ in 1 L cylinders.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "2.2 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Calibration procedure",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Each cylinder was wetted to eight water contents between air-dry and saturation and equilibrated for 24 h. The probe voltage ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "V",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " was recorded for 5 min, after which a 20 mL core was weighed wet and oven-dried at 105 °C to obtain the volumetric water content ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\theta",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ". We fitted the quadratic model",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\theta =a_{0}+a_{1}V+a_{2}V^{2}",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "by least squares, either separately for each soil or for all 144 samples together. Goodness of fit is reported as the coefficient of determination ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "R^{2}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " and the RMSE of ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "(2)",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\mathrm{RMSE}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}{{\\left(\\theta_{i}^{\\mathrm{model}}-\\theta_{i}^{\\mathrm{ref}}\\right)}^{2}}}",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Temperature drift was measured by cycling the loam cylinders between 278 and 293 K in a climate chamber at constant water content.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "3 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": " lists the fit quality. All soils are described well by Eq. (",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "): ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "R^{2}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " exceeds 0.95 everywhere and the bias is within ±0.003 m³ m⁻³. The error grows with clay content, from 0.012 m³ m⁻³ in the sand to 0.021 m³ m⁻³ in the clay. The pooled model has an RMSE of 0.019 m³ m⁻³.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Soil",
          "n",
          "R²",
          "RMSE (m³ m⁻³)",
          "Bias (m³ m⁻³)"
        ],
        [
          "Sand",
          "48",
          "0.981",
          "0.012",
          "0.001"
        ],
        [
          "Loam",
          "48",
          "0.972",
          "0.015",
          "−0.002"
        ],
        [
          "Clay",
          "48",
          "0.951",
          "0.021",
          "0.003"
        ],
        [
          "Pooled",
          "144",
          "0.962",
          "0.019",
          "0.000"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "",
      "label": null,
      "alignment": "",
      "style": "",
      "merges": [],
      "header": false,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Table 1: ",
          "marks": [
            "bold",
            "italic"
          ],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Fit of Eq. (1) to the calibration data, per soil and for all soils pooled.",
          "marks": [
            "italic"
          ],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "center",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Between 278 and 293 K the output of the loam cylinders drifted by less than 0.004 m³ m⁻³, smaller than the calibration error and therefore negligible for irrigation decisions.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "4 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Discussion",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A sand-specific model lowers the RMSE of the pooled model (0.019 m³ m⁻³) to 0.012 m³ m⁻³, a 37% reduction, whereas the clay-specific model (0.021 m³ m⁻³) is no better than the pooled one. This agrees with the explanation that the dielectric response of bound water in clay is the dominant error source ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "[2]",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Salinity was not varied here and is the main limitation: in saline soils the probes read high",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ".",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "5 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Conclusions",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A 3 USD capacitive probe reaches an RMSE of 0.012 to 0.021 m³ m⁻³ after a quadratic, soil-specific calibration. A pooled calibration is sufficient for coarse scheduling (0.019 m³ m⁻³), but texture-specific models should be used when the error budget is tighter. Future work will add salinity as a second predictor.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "CRediT authorship contribution statement",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
