        {
          "text": "Corresponding author: t.marren@example.org",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Correspondence"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Event pipelines must discard duplicate messages without keeping every identifier in memory. We describe a membership filter that stores short fingerprints in a table of small buckets, where each key may live in one of two buckets. We prove a closed-form bound on the false-positive rate, show that with 12-bit fingerprints the structure needs about 12.6 bits per key at 95% load, and report that a prototype drops 99.8% of synthetic duplicates at 2.1 million events per second on a single core.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Abstract"
    },
    {
      "children": [
