{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nitrate retention in restored headwater wetlands depends on residence time, not on wetland age",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Restoring wetlands in agricultural headwaters is promoted as a way to cut nitrate export, but field evidence on how much they retain is mixed. We measured nitrate-nitrogen at the inlet and outlet of three restored wetlands over one hydrological year. Annual retention was 29 to 46% of inflow concentration. A first-order removal model with a single rate constant of 0.12 to 0.14 per day reproduced all three sites, which indicates that hydraulic residence time, rather than wetland age or area, controls retention. Managing outflow to lengthen residence time in the wet season is therefore the most direct lever for improving performance.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Abstract"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "wetland restoration · nitrate · residence time · catchment management",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Keywords"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:intro",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nitrate leaching from fertilised fields degrades downstream water quality and drives eutrophication of estuaries ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "lund16"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Constructed and restored wetlands intercept drainage and remove nitrate by plant uptake and denitrification, the microbial reduction of nitrate to nitrogen gas. Reported removal ranges widely, from a few per cent to well over half of the inflowing load ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "wei18",
            "lund16"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ", and it is unclear which design variable explains the spread.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Here we test whether a single parameter, the hydraulic residence time ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\tau",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ", explains retention across three restored wetlands of different age and size in one catchment.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Methods",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:methods",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Study sites",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The three wetlands (A to C) drain pasture and arable fields in a 14 km",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": [
            "superscript"
          ],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": " catchment. They were restored 3 years (A), 7 years (B) and 11 years (C) before the study, and cover 0.8, 1.4 and 0.5 ha.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Sampling and analysis",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Inlet and outlet were sampled weekly from October to September with flow-proportional autosamplers. Nitrate-nitrogen was measured by ion chromatography, and flow by a calibrated V-notch weir. Residence time is the mean wetland water volume ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "V",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " divided by mean discharge ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "Q",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Retention is the relative drop in concentration,",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "R=\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{in}}-C_{\\mathrm{out}}}{C_{\\mathrm{in}}},\\qquad \\tau=\\frac{V}{Q},",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:retention",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "and a plug-flow wetland with first-order removal predicts",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "R=1-e^{-k\\tau},\\qquad k=-\\frac{\\ln(1-R)}{\\tau},",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:firstorder",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "where ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the removal rate constant in ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\mathrm{d}^{-1}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ".",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:results",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "All wetlands retained nitrate throughout the year, although retention fell during storm flows when ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\tau",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " dropped below a day. Annual values are in Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:wetlands",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Retention ranged from 28.6% at site C to 45.8% at site B.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Site",
          "In (mg/L)",
          "Out (mg/L)",
          "R (per cent)",
          "Residence (d)",
          "k (1/d)"
        ],
        [
          "A",
          "3.2",
          "1.9",
          "40.6",
          "4.2",
          "0.12"
        ],
        [
          "B",
          "4.8",
          "2.6",
          "45.8",
          "5.1",
          "0.12"
        ],
        [
          "C",
          "2.1",
          "1.5",
          "28.6",
          "2.4",
          "0.14"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Annual mean nitrate-nitrogen at inlet and outlet, retention R, mean residence time and rate constant k.",
      "label": "tab:wetlands",
      "alignment": "lrrrrr",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "merges": [],
      "header": true,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Applying ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:firstorder",
          "kind": "eqref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " to each site gives ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " between 0.12 and 0.14 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\mathrm{d}^{-1}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ", a spread of less than 20% around the mean of 0.13, although the wetlands differ threefold in area and almost fourfold in age. Retention tracks residence time: the site with the longest ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\tau",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " retained the most.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Discussion",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:disc",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The similar rate constants suggest that removal per unit time is a property of the catchment's temperature, carbon supply and flora, which the three sites share, rather than of the age of the wetland. This agrees with the view that wetland performance stabilises within a few years of restoration ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "wei18"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ".",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Two caveats apply. First, three sites cannot separate residence time from other site traits. Second, sampling was weekly, so short storm pulses are under-represented. Management implications are:",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "keep outlets adjustable so that residence time can be raised in the wet season;",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "design for a residence time above about 5 days, which gave 45% retention here;",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "prioritise sites with a large share of the catchment's drainage over additional area.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        }
      ],
      "type": "List"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Conclusions",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:concl",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nitrate retention in three restored headwater wetlands followed a single first-order law in the residence time. Operating wetlands to maximise residence time is the most practical way to raise their performance.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Retention is defined on concentrations; a load-based estimate would also account for water lost to evapotranspiration.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "Footnote"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Acknowledgements",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": false,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We thank the landowners for access and the Lowmoor Catchment Trust for field support.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
