{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Does Generous Line Height Help? Reading Speed and Comfort on Phone Screens",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Rosa Salgado",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "University of Northfield",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Affiliation"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Idris Ketterman",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Lowmoor Design Studio",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Affiliation"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Phone interfaces often set body text with tight line spacing to fit more words on screen. We ran a within-subjects study with 36 participants who read three short articles, each set with a line height of 1.2, 1.4 or 1.6 times the font size. Reading speed differed across conditions (F(2, 70) = 6.84, p = .002): the 1.4 setting was read about 5% faster than the tightest one, and participants rated it as most comfortable. Going from 1.4 to 1.6 brought no further gain. We discuss defaults for mobile reading interfaces.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Abstract"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:intro",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "People read long texts on phones more than ever, yet layout defaults are tuned for screenshots rather than for reading. Line height is a cheap parameter to change: classic typographic advice favours generous leading for long lines ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "brandt12"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ", but phone lines are short, and the benefit is unclear. Prior work on small screens compared font sizes and column widths ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "osei17"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": " rather than vertical spacing.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We ask two questions:",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Does line height change reading speed on a phone when comprehension is held constant?",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Which line height do readers prefer?",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        }
      ],
      "type": "List"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Related Work",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:related",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Studies of print typography found that very tight leading slows readers because the eye drifts to the neighbouring line ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "brandt12"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". On screens the effect depends on line length: Lindgren and Aziz report that the optimum moves towards tighter spacing as lines get shorter ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "lindgren15"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ", which is the regime of phones. Work on mobile reading has focused on how text is delivered, for example rapid serial presentation, rather than on its static layout ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "osei17",
            "nakano20"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". We complement it with a controlled test of one layout parameter.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Method",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:method",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Participants and design",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We recruited 36 adults (20 women, 15 men, 1 not disclosed; ages 19 to 58, median 31) through a university mailing list. All read English as a first language and had normal or corrected vision. Each participant read three articles of about 450 words (Flesch reading ease 62 to 66) in one of the three line heights. Condition order and article assignment were counterbalanced with a Latin square.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Apparatus and procedure",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Texts were shown in a web page on a 6.1-inch phone at a 16 px serif font, with 32-character lines. Participants scrolled at their own pace; scroll events gave the reading time. After each article they answered four multiple-choice questions and rated comfort on a seven-point scale. Reading speed is words divided by reading time, ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "v=\\frac{N}{t}\\cdot 60",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ", in words per minute (wpm).",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We analysed speed only for articles on which at least three of the four questions were answered correctly, which excluded 7 of 108 readings. The omnibus test was followed by paired comparisons with a Holm correction.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "sec:results",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Comprehension was high and did not differ between conditions (about 3.4 of 4 questions in each). Mean reading speed is shown in Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:speed",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ". A repeated-measures ANOVA found a main effect of line height, ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "F(2,70)=6.84",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ", ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "p=.002",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ", ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\eta_p^{2}=.16",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ".",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Line height",
          "Speed (wpm)",
          "SD",
          "Comfort (1-7)"
        ],
        [
          "1.2",
          "231",
          "38",
          "4.6"
        ],
        [
          "1.4",
          "243",
          "41",
          "5.5"
        ],
        [
          "1.6",
          "240",
          "40",
          "5.2"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Mean reading speed and comfort by line height (N = 36).",
      "label": "tab:speed",
      "alignment": "lrrr",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "merges": [],
      "header": true,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Relative to the 1.2 setting, the 1.4 setting was ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "(243-231)/231\\approx 5\\%",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " faster and the 1.6 setting ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "(240-231)/231\\approx 4\\%",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " faster. The two roomier settings did not differ from each other. Comfort followed the same order, and when asked to pick a favourite, 22 participants chose 1.4, 9 chose 1.6 and 5 chose 1.2.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Reading speed and comfort were only weakly related (Pearson ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "r=.21",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": "), so the preference for 1.4 is not simply a matter of reading faster.",
