{
  "children": [
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Reading the Weather",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Title"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Fiona Maclaren",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Barometer and the Sky",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "ch:baro",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Long before satellites, sailors read the weather from two things: the height of the mercury and the shape of the clouds. Both still work. This chapter explains why, and how to combine them into a forecast you can trust for the next six hours.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\marginnote{All pressures in this book are in hectopascals (hPa). Standard sea-level pressure is 1013.25 hPa.}",
          "type": "InlineRaw"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "What a barometer measures",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "A barometer measures the weight of the air above it. Pressure falls with height roughly exponentially, following the barometric formula",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "p(h) = p_0\\, e^{-h/H}, \\qquad H \\approx 8.4\\ \\mathrm{km},",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:baro",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "where ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "p_0",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the pressure at sea level and ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "H",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the scale height",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The scale height depends on temperature, so the real atmosphere is a little more complicated; 8.4 km suits typical mid-latitude conditions.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "Footnote"
        },
        {
          "text": ". At the summit of a 1344 m hill Equation ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:baro",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " predicts a pressure about 15 per cent lower than at the foot.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "What matters for forecasting is not the absolute reading but its change. Meteorologists call this the pressure tendency, and Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:tend",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " gives the standard rules of thumb.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\marginnote{Always correct the reading to sea level before you compare it with a published chart.}",
          "type": "InlineRaw"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Tendency in 3 hours",
          "Likely weather"
        ],
        [
          "Rising by more than 6 hPa",
          "Clearing, then windy"
        ],
        [
          "Rising slowly",
          "Settled, fair"
        ],
        [
          "Steady",
          "No change"
        ],
        [
          "Falling slowly",
          "Cloud and light rain later"
        ],
        [
          "Falling by more than 6 hPa",
          "Gale or heavy rain within hours"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Pressure tendency and the weather that usually follows.",
      "label": "tab:tend",
      "alignment": "ll",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "merges": [],
      "header": true,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Reading the clouds",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "High, thin clouds that arrive from the west often precede a warm front by a day. They thicken, lower, and darken as rain approaches. By contrast, towering cumulus that builds in the afternoon on a hot day warns of a shower, not a change of air mass.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\marginnote{Mackerel sky and mares' tails: the old rhyme says that they make tall ships carry low sails.}",
          "type": "InlineRaw"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Wind and the Rule of Buys Ballot",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "ch:wind",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Wind is air moving from high pressure to low, but the spin of the Earth bends its path. In the northern hemisphere the result is simple to state: stand with your back to the wind and low pressure is on your left. This rule, named after a Dutch meteorologist, lets you locate a depression from a single wind observation.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The strength of the wind",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Wind speed follows the pressure gradient. Closely spaced isobars, as shown on any chart, mean strong wind. The balance between the pressure-gradient and Coriolis forces gives the geostrophic wind,",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "v_g = \\frac{1}{\\rho f}\\,\\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta n},",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "eq:geo",
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "where ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\rho",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is air density, ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "f",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " the Coriolis parameter, and ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\Delta p/\\Delta n",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " the pressure change across a distance ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\Delta n",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " perpendicular to the isobars. At 55 degrees north, a gradient of 4 hPa over 100 km gives a geostrophic wind of about 27 m/s in Equation ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:geo",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ", which is the strong gale of Beaufort force 10.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\marginnote{1 m/s is about 1.94 knots. Force 10 spans 24.5 to 28.4 m/s.}",
          "type": "InlineRaw"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Near the surface, friction slows the wind and turns it a little towards low pressure, so real winds blow across the isobars at around 20 degrees over the sea and 35 degrees over land.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "title": "Reading the Weather",
    "author": "Fiona Maclaren",
    "documentclass": "tufte-book",
    "class_options": "",
    "packages": [
      "amsmath",
      "amssymb",
      "graphicx",
      "multicol",
      "float"
    ],
    "page_size": "A4",
    "margin_top_cm": 2.5,
    "margin_bottom_cm": 2.5,
    "margin_left_cm": 2.5,
    "margin_right_cm": 2.5,
    "body_font_pt": 10,
    "body_font_family": "default",
    "visual_font_family": "Georgia",
    "line_spacing": 1.0,
    "paragraph_indent": false,
    "column_count": 1,
    "frontmatter_extras": "",
    "preamble_extras": "",
    "ref_library": "",
    "bib_style": "plainnat"
  },
  "type": "Document"
}

