    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "ch:intro",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The harbour as a vantage point",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Travel writing on the Mediterranean is usually read for its ruins. Yet the writers examined here arrive by sea, and the first thing they describe is not a temple but a waterfront crowded with ships, customs sheds and interpreters ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "moretti2009",
            "bianchi2014"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The approach from the water fixes a particular point of view: the city is seen whole, from outside, before it is entered.",
          "marks": [],
