        {
          "text": "The harbour as a vantage point",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Travel writing on the Mediterranean is usually read for its ruins. Yet the writers examined here arrive by sea, and the first thing they describe is not a temple but a waterfront crowded with ships, customs sheds and interpreters ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "[1, 2]",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The approach from the water fixes a particular point of view: the city is seen whole, from outside, before it is entered.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "1.2 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Corpus and method",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The corpus consists of six narratives, listed in Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": ", chosen because each devotes at least one chapter to a single port. The method is close reading, supported by a simple count of the commercial vocabulary in each text.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Narrative (fictional)",
