      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Definition (Cosmopolitan scene). ",
          "marks": [
            "bold",
            "italic"
          ],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "A passage in which three or more languages or communities are described as sharing one public space.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "By this definition the narratives contain 41 cosmopolitan scenes in about 1,900 pages, a density that falls sharply in the two last texts of the corpus. The change tracks the hardening of national categories after 1860, and supports the argument of the introduction.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "3 ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "Conclusion",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Reading from the harbour changes what counts as evidence: the customs list becomes as eloquent as the cathedral. Further work could extend the corpus to Greek and Arabic sources, which would test whether the same vantage point organizes accounts written from the shore.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
