        {
          "text": "Simulation results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": "ch:results",
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The controller was tested on a 12 km urban cycle with 38 stops. Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:results",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " compares the vehicle without recovery, a fixed blending gain and the proposed slip-based law.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Strategy",
          "Energy recovered (per cent)",
          "Range (km)",
          "Stop from 30 km/h (m)"
        ],
        [
          "No recovery",
          "0",
          "62",
          "6.9"
        ],
        [
          "Fixed gain 0.5",
          "9.7",
          "67",
          "7.4"
        ],
        [
          "Slip-based law",
          "14.8",
          "71",
          "7.6"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Urban-cycle results for three braking strategies.",
      "label": "tab:results",
      "alignment": "lrrr",
      "style": "booktabs",
      "merges": [],
      "header": true,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The slip-based law meets all three specifications: it recovers 14.8 per cent of the energy, against the 12 per cent target, and stops in 7.6 m, inside the 8 m limit. The peak charge current was 37 A.",
          "marks": [],
