        }
      ],
      "type": "Affiliation"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "ZnO nanoparticles were synthesised by a sol–gel route and calcined at 400, 600 and 800 °C. Powder X-ray diffraction confirms single-phase wurtzite (P6₃mc) with a = 3.2498 Å and c = 5.2066 Å. Crystallite sizes from the Scherrer equation (12–39 nm) are compared with a Williamson–Hall analysis (17–48 nm) that separates size and microstrain contributions to the line broadening; the microstrain falls from 2.3 × 10⁻³ to 0.4 × 10⁻³ and the grain-growth activation energy is about 32 kJ mol⁻¹.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Abstract"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "XRD · Scherrer · Williamson–Hall · ZnO · nanoparticles",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Keywords"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Line broadening in powder diffraction carries information on both the size of coherently scattering domains and on inhomogeneous strain ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
